.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n4","ref":"3.1","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"childrenOf at ux.ts:181-188 encodes the nesting rule as exactly THREE container cases — items for Container, items for Column, columns for ColumnSet — with every other variant returning an empty list.","evidence":"CONFIRMED verbatim. ux.ts:181 declares childrenOf, :183 `case 'Container': return el.items;`, :184 `case 'Column':    return el.items;`, :185 `case 'ColumnSet': return el.columns;`, :186 `default:          return [];`. Exactly three container cases, everything else empty — as the LLD states.","action":"No change. This matters because the LLD requires the new emitter to reuse this nesting rule rather than re-derive it, which is what keeps order and nesting correct by construction.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n5","ref":"2.1","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"renderUxCompanion at render.ts:131 ALREADY returns kind:'ux-mock' — it does not stamp a diagram kind — so the ref vocabulary needs no change and no consumer is affected. This is the LLD's stated correction to the approved ISSUE's triage signal.","evidence":"CONFIRMED, and this is the LLD's most consequential correction. render.ts:131 `export async function renderUxCompanion(`, :137 `const ir = withSourceLink(uxDefinitionToIr(uxDef), opts.sourceLink);`, :138 `const outcome = await assembleShell(ir);`. The returned kind is at render.ts:151 — `kind:    'ux-mock',` — the ONLY occurrence of that literal in source. The approved ISSUE's triage signal claimed this function 'currently stamps a diagram kind'; it does not.","action":"No change to the design. One small accuracy note: the LLD's open question cites this as 'render.ts:151' while the grep resolves the line to :151. Immaterial — the claim is that the ref already carries kind:'ux-mock', which is confirmed — but it is the second one-off anchor in the artifact, alongside n1's.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n6","ref":"6","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"assembleShell at src/docgen/render/shell.ts:255 is unconditionally a DIAGRAM shell: it loads the mermaid runtime and, when oversized(ir), falls back to toGraphvizDot(ir) rendered as SVG at :270. Neither branch lays out a document.","evidence":"CONFIRMED verbatim. shell.ts:255 `export async function assembleShell(`, :269 `if (oversized(ir)) {`, :270 `const result = await renderer.renderSvg(toGraphvizDot(ir));`. Both branches are graph renderers — the mermaid runtime load and the graphviz subprocess fallback — so the LLD's claim that this function has no layout path, and that a UX IR 'could not render as a mock even in principle', holds.","action":"No change.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n7","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"assembleShell is depended on by THREE other companions besides UX — the ER, sequence and component renderers in src/workflow/artifacts/companion/render.ts — which is the blast-radius fact that eliminated alternative a3.","evidence":"CONFIRMED, and I re-derived it directly because the engine's grep hit the 50-match cap and returned zero companion-module hits. src/workflow/artifacts/companion/render.ts calls assembleShell at FOUR sites — :91, :138, :187 and :225 — one per companion, matching the four render functions declared at :84 renderErCompanion, :131 renderUxCompanion, :180 renderSequenceCompanion and :225 renderComponentCompanion. So removing UX from that seam leaves exactly THREE other dependents, precisely as the LLD states.","action":"No change. This is the constraint (b1) that eliminated alternative a3, so it being exact matters more than most — and it is.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n8","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"context-sections.test.ts:82 pins that an EMPTY card body still yields narrated section titles of exactly ['Purpose','Legend'] — the prose contract the fix must preserve.","evidence":"CONFIRMED verbatim at context-sections.test.ts:82. The surrounding lines show the same assertion for the er (:81), sequence (:83) and component (:84) mappers, so ['Purpose','Legend'] is a uniform contract across all four companions — which strengthens the LLD's invariant rather than weakening it: breaking it on the UX path would make UX the odd one out.","action":"No change.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n9","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"ux-integration.test.ts pins the link-not-inline invariant twice, at :75 and :124, each asserting the card JSON is never inlined into the core markdown.","evidence":"CONFIRMED verbatim at both anchors. ux-integration.test.ts:75 and :124 each read `assert.ok(!md.includes('\"AdaptiveCard\"'), 'the card JSON is never inlined into the core markdown');`. The link-not-inline invariant is pinned exactly twice, as the LLD says.","action":"No change.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n10","ref":"5","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"Existing fixtures already exercise an Input.Text alongside a TextBlock — ux-render.test.ts:29-30 and ux-integration.test.ts:43-44 — so input rendering is testable against cards that predate the fix.","evidence":"CONFIRMED verbatim. ux-render.test.ts:29-30 are `{ type: 'TextBlock', text: 'Filter tags' },` and `{ type: 'Input.Text', id: 'q', label: 'Query' },`; ux-integration.test.ts:43-44 are the same shape with `id: 'customer', label: 'Customer'`. Input rendering is therefore testable against fixtures that predate the fix, exactly as the test strategy claims.","action":"No change.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n11","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"No Adaptive Cards RENDERER is a dependency anywhere — only the JSON schema ships, loaded by loadAdaptiveCardsSchema for ajv validation — so alternative a2 would have been net-new dependency weight rather than adoption of something present.","evidence":"CONFIRMED by absence, which is the point. A grep for 'adaptivecards' returns ZERO matches anywhere in the repo, while loadAdaptiveCardsSchema resolves to adaptive-cards.ts:46 and is consumed at ux.ts:26 and ux.ts:153 for validation. So the repo carries the Adaptive Cards SCHEMA and no renderer — alternative a2 would have been net-new dependency weight, as the LLD scored it.","action":"No change.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n12","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"The proposed new symbol renderUxMockDocument does not exist anywhere in the source tree today, so the LLD declares it as new rather than asserting an existing API.","evidence":"CONFIRMED. `renderUxMockDocument` returns TWO matches, both in docs/ prose (this LLD and its generated companion) and ZERO in source. The name is genuinely new and collides with nothing, so declaring it as a `new` dataModel entity rather than asserting it in the api section was the correct call.","action":"No change.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n13","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The ~3.37MB companion size the LLD cites is real and current: the ux-mock.html this very LLD generated measures 3,370,121 bytes, and Epic bfe98ff7's measured 3,371,391 — both produced by the diagram path the fix replaces.","evidence":"CONFIRMED by direct measurement rather than by the probe, which was a poor choice on my part — 'mermaid' over src/ cannot measure a generated artifact in docs/. Measured directly: the ux-mock.html this LLD itself generated is 3,370,121 bytes and Epic bfe98ff7's is 3,371,391 bytes, both produced by the diagram path. The ~3.37MB figure the LLD cites is current, not historical.","action":"No change. Worth noting the artifact now carries its OWN before-state: this LLD generated a 3.37MB node-diagram mock of a card designed to exercise all eight variants, which makes regenerating that exact file the honest after-shot once the fix lands — and the LLD's migration step 5 already calls for precisely that.","fixability":"auto"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":0,"low":13},"reviewedAt":"2026-10-01T12:14:54.418Z","model":"client"},"resolutions":{}}}}